[TEXT]
STATEMENT: >>>
As-built detail of the four flight-dynamics checks: `flightmd_core/analysers/oscillation.py`, `vibration.py`,
`ekf.py` and `ascent_profile.py`. Each statement gives the function, its inputs and outputs and the constants
with the numbers held in the code. Timestamps are log-relative microseconds unless a unit is given. "In flight"
and "armed" mean inside the windows returned by `flight_phases` (the whole log when it has no arming data).
`confidence(margin, duration, quality)` is the helper in `analysers/helpers.py`: it returns
(0.5 + 0.5 x margin limited to 0..1) x (1 with no duration, else 0.4 + 0.6 x duration/2 s limited to 0..1) x
(quality limited to 0..1), rounded to 3 places. The design is in `06-design/lld-dyn.md`.
<<<

[[SECTION]]
TITLE: Oscillation (flightmd_core/analysers/oscillation.py)

[REQUIREMENT]
UID: LLR-DYN-001
STATUS: Active
TITLE: Oscillation module declaration
STATEMENT: >>>
`OscillationAnalyser` declares `name` `"oscillation"`, `display_name` `"Oscillation Analysis"`,
`required_topics` `["vehicle_angular_velocity"]` and `weight` 20.0, so `is_applicable` returns False for a log
without that topic.
<<<
VERIFICATION: Test
ALLOCATION: analysers
RELATIONS:
- TYPE: Parent
  VALUE: HLR-DYN-001

[REQUIREMENT]
UID: LLR-DYN-002
STATUS: Active
TITLE: Oscillation skip versus assessed
STATEMENT: >>>
`OscillationAnalyser.analyse` returns `_skip("Angular velocity topic has no timestamps.")` when the topic has no
`timestamp` column. For each axis it drops rows with NaN in `timestamp` or the axis column, sorts by time, keeps
the in-air rows and takes the sample rate as 1e6 divided by the median interval (0.0 with fewer than two rows or
a median that is not positive). An axis is not assessed when the rate is below `MIN_RATE_HZ` = 10.0 Hz or when
`_spectrogram` returns None. When no axis is assessed it returns a skip: "Angular rate is logged at <rate> Hz;
oscillation analysis needs at least 10 Hz." if any axis was below the rate (which includes a rate of 0.0 from
fewer than two in-flight rows), otherwise "Not enough angular-rate data (<phase>) for a 4 s analysis window."
When at least one axis is assessed it returns an assessed result even if the other axes were not.
<<<
VERIFICATION: Test
ALLOCATION: analysers
RELATIONS:
- TYPE: Parent
  VALUE: HLR-DYN-001

[REQUIREMENT]
UID: LLR-DYN-003
STATUS: Active
TITLE: Rate column names
STATEMENT: >>>
`OscillationAnalyser.analyse` takes the roll rate from the first present of `rollspeed`, `xyz[0]`, `x`, the
pitch rate from `pitchspeed`, `xyz[1]`, `y` and the yaw rate from `yawspeed`, `xyz[2]`, `z`, and leaves out an
axis with none of its columns. Values are taken to be rad/s.
<<<
VERIFICATION: Test
ALLOCATION: analysers
RELATIONS:
- TYPE: Parent
  VALUE: HLR-DYN-001

[REQUIREMENT]
UID: LLR-DYN-004
STATUS: Active
TITLE: Window length, gap splitting and resampling
STATEMENT: >>>
`_spectrogram` sets the window length to 2 to the power round(log2(`SEGMENT_S` x rate)) samples with `SEGMENT_S`
= 4.0 s (1024 samples at 250 Hz, 32 at 10 Hz; between 2.83 s and 5.66 s). It splits the samples wherever two
consecutive timestamps are more than `GAP_FACTOR` = 5.0 nominal intervals apart, skips a stretch with fewer
samples than one window or whose uniform grid (first to last timestamp, step 1e6/rate microseconds, end
excluded) is shorter than one window, and interpolates each kept stretch linearly onto its grid. It returns None
when no stretch is kept.
<<<
VERIFICATION: Test
ALLOCATION: analysers
RELATIONS:
- TYPE: Parent
  VALUE: HLR-DYN-002
- TYPE: Parent
  VALUE: HLR-DYN-001

[REQUIREMENT]
UID: LLR-DYN-005
STATUS: Active
TITLE: Spectrogram parameters and band
STATEMENT: >>>
`_spectrogram` computes `scipy.signal.spectrogram` on each stretch with a Hann window, `nperseg` equal to the
window length, `noverlap` of half the window, `detrend="constant"`, `scaling="density"` and `mode="psd"`, and
joins the columns of all stretches. The window centre times are the grid start plus the spectrogram times. The
analysed band is every frequency bin from `FREQ_LOW` = 1.0 Hz to the lower of `FREQ_HIGH_CAP` = 100.0 Hz and
0.8 x rate / 2. The result also holds the bin width in Hz, the rate and the window length in microseconds.
<<<
VERIFICATION: Test, Inspection
VERIFICATION_NOTE: >>>
Tests check that a known sine is found within 1 Hz and with the right amplitude. The window, overlap, detrend
and band values are checked by inspection of the call.
<<<
ALLOCATION: analysers
RELATIONS:
- TYPE: Parent
  VALUE: HLR-DYN-002

[REQUIREMENT]
UID: LLR-DYN-006
STATUS: Active
TITLE: Peak, prominence and amplitude per window
STATEMENT: >>>
For each window `_worst` takes the band bin with the highest PSD as the peak, the median PSD of the band as the
floor, and the prominence as peak PSD divided by the floor (floor not below 1e-30). The amplitude in deg/s is
`degrees(sqrt(2 x p))` where p is the sum over the peak bin and two bins each side of the PSD above the floor
(negative differences taken as 0) times the bin width. The amplitude of a window whose prominence is below
`MIN_PROMINENCE` = 25.0 is set to 0.
<<<
VERIFICATION: Test
ALLOCATION: analysers
RELATIONS:
- TYPE: Parent
  VALUE: HLR-DYN-002

[REQUIREMENT]
UID: LLR-DYN-007
STATUS: Active
TITLE: Sustained value over three windows
STATEMENT: >>>
`_worst` smooths the per-window amplitude and prominence each with a centred rolling median over 3 windows
(`min_periods` 1, so the first and last window use two values and a single-window log uses one). It selects the
window with the highest smoothed amplitude and returns that smoothed amplitude as `amp_dps` and the smoothed
prominence of the same window as `prominence`. `peak_hz` is the peak frequency of the selected window when the
smoothed amplitude is above 0, otherwise the band frequency where the PSD averaged over all windows is highest.
<<<
VERIFICATION: Test
ALLOCATION: analysers
RELATIONS:
- TYPE: Parent
  VALUE: HLR-DYN-002
- TYPE: Parent
  VALUE: HLR-DYN-003

[REQUIREMENT]
UID: LLR-DYN-008
STATUS: Active
TITLE: Time span of an oscillation
STATEMENT: >>>
`_worst` marks every window whose unsmoothed amplitude is `AMP_MILD` = 5.0 deg/s or more and whose peak is
within 2.0 Hz of `peak_hz`. `t0` is the earliest marked centre time less half a window and `t1` the latest plus
half a window (both None when no window is marked). `duration_s` is the number of marked windows times half the
window length in seconds.
<<<
VERIFICATION: Test
ALLOCATION: analysers
RELATIONS:
- TYPE: Parent
  VALUE: HLR-DYN-002
- TYPE: Parent
  VALUE: HLR-DYN-004

[REQUIREMENT]
UID: LLR-DYN-009
STATUS: Active
TITLE: Oscillation level
STATEMENT: >>>
`_level` returns `"NONE"` when `prominence` is below `MIN_PROMINENCE` = 25.0; otherwise `"SEVERE"` when
`amp_dps` is `AMP_SEVERE` = 40.0 deg/s or more, `"MODERATE"` at `AMP_MODERATE` = 15.0 or more, `"MILD"` at
`AMP_MILD` = 5.0 or more, and `"NONE"` below that. A NaN prominence or amplitude gives `"NONE"`.
<<<
VERIFICATION: Test
ALLOCATION: analysers
RELATIONS:
- TYPE: Parent
  VALUE: HLR-DYN-003

[REQUIREMENT]
UID: LLR-DYN-010
STATUS: Active
TITLE: Oscillation module score
STATEMENT: >>>
`OscillationAnalyser.analyse` starts `health_score` at 100.0 and for each axis whose level is not `"NONE"`
subtracts `PENALTIES`: 30 for `SEVERE`, 15 for `MODERATE`, 5 for `MILD`, not going below 0.0, and adds one
finding for that axis. An assessed log with no such axis returns 100.0 and no findings.
<<<
VERIFICATION: Test
ALLOCATION: analysers
RELATIONS:
- TYPE: Parent
  VALUE: HLR-DYN-003

[REQUIREMENT]
UID: LLR-DYN-011
STATUS: Active
TITLE: Oscillation key metrics
STATEMENT: >>>
For every assessed axis `OscillationAnalyser.analyse` writes three key metrics whether or not a finding fires:
`<axis>_peak_hz` (Hz, 3 places), `<axis>_norm_amp` (the prominence, 3 places) and `<axis>_osc_amp_dps` (deg/s,
2 places), with `<axis>` one of `roll`, `pitch`, `yaw`.
<<<
VERIFICATION: Test
ALLOCATION: analysers
RELATIONS:
- TYPE: Parent
  VALUE: HLR-DYN-004

[REQUIREMENT]
UID: LLR-DYN-012
STATUS: Active
TITLE: Spectrum chart
STATEMENT: >>>
`_chart` returns `frequencies` (Hz, from `CHART_FREQ_MIN` = 0.5 Hz up) and `amplitudes` (square root of the PSD
averaged over all windows, rad/s per root Hz) of equal length, taking every n-th bin so that at most
`CHART_MAX_BINS` = 512 remain, with non-finite values as None. The finding's `chart_data` adds `axis`,
`peak_hz`, `norm_amp` and `amp_dps`.
<<<
VERIFICATION: Test
ALLOCATION: analysers
RELATIONS:
- TYPE: Parent
  VALUE: HLR-DYN-004

[REQUIREMENT]
UID: LLR-DYN-013
STATUS: Active
TITLE: Oscillation finding: severity, kind and text
STATEMENT: >>>
`_finding` maps `SEVERE` to critical, `MODERATE` to warning and `MILD` to info. `kind` is `oscillation.rate_p`
when `peak_hz` is below `MID_BAND_HZ` = 12.0, `oscillation.rate_d` when it is below `HIGH_BAND_HZ` = 35.0, and
`oscillation.mechanical` otherwise. The title is "<Axis>-Axis Oscillation at <Hz> Hz"; `technical_summary` and
`plain_english` are the same sentence giving frequency, amplitude, level and its threshold, prominence, duration,
phase description and sample rate. The category is `oscillation`.
<<<
VERIFICATION: Test
ALLOCATION: analysers
RELATIONS:
- TYPE: Parent
  VALUE: HLR-DYN-004
- TYPE: Parent
  VALUE: HLR-DYN-005

[REQUIREMENT]
UID: LLR-DYN-014
STATUS: Active
TITLE: Rate P recommendation
STATEMENT: >>>
In the P band `_finding` looks up the P gain name for the vehicle and axis. When the name exists and the
parameter set holds it with a value above 0 it adds one `ParamRecommendation`: `current_value` rounded to 6
places, `suggested_value` = `round(current x 0.87, 4)`, `change_direction` `"decrease"`, and the recommendation
text "Reduce <name> from <current> to <new> (about 13%), then re-test." The suggested value has no lower or
upper bound and is not checked against firmware metadata; it is 0.0 for a current value below about 0.0000575
and equals the current value for some values below 0.0004. With no name, a missing parameter, or a value that is
0, negative or NaN, no `ParamRecommendation` is made and the text is "Lower the <axis> rate P gain by about
13%."
<<<
VERIFICATION: Test, Inspection
VERIFICATION_NOTE: >>>
Tests check the name, a lower suggested value, the direction, the text and that no value is made up for a
missing parameter. The rounding, the absence of bounds and the zero/negative/NaN cases are checked by
inspection.
<<<
ALLOCATION: analysers
RELATIONS:
- TYPE: Parent
  VALUE: HLR-DYN-005

[REQUIREMENT]
UID: LLR-DYN-015
STATUS: Active
TITLE: Rate D recommendation
STATEMENT: >>>
In the D band `_finding` looks up the D gain name. When the name exists and the parameter set holds it with a
value above 0 it adds one `ParamRecommendation`: `current_value` rounded to 6 places, `suggested_value` =
`round(current x 0.85, 5)`, `change_direction` `"decrease"`, and the text "Reduce <name> from <current> to
<new> (about 15%), or lower the D-term filter cutoff. Don't raise D for this one." The suggested value has no
bound and is 0.0 for a current value below about 0.0000059. Otherwise no `ParamRecommendation` is made and the
text is "Lower the <axis> rate D gain by about 15%, or lower the D-term filter cutoff.", also for a vehicle
with no D gain name.
<<<
VERIFICATION: Test, Inspection
VERIFICATION_NOTE: >>>
One test checks a 22 Hz peak on a PX4 multirotor gives one decrease of `MC_ROLLRATE_D`. The factor, rounding and
the generic text are checked by inspection.
<<<
ALLOCATION: analysers
RELATIONS:
- TYPE: Parent
  VALUE: HLR-DYN-005

[REQUIREMENT]
UID: LLR-DYN-016
STATUS: Active
TITLE: Gain names by vehicle and what is withheld
STATEMENT: >>>
`_finding` takes the gain names from `rate_gain_names(vehicle_from_params(params), axis)`: PX4 multirotor
`MC_ROLLRATE_P/D`, `MC_PITCHRATE_P/D`, `MC_YAWRATE_P/D`; PX4 fixed wing `FW_RR_P`, `FW_PR_P`, `FW_YR_P` with no
D gain; ArduPilot multirotor `ATC_RAT_RLL_P/D`, `ATC_RAT_PIT_P/D`, `ATC_RAT_YAW_P/D`; ArduPilot fixed wing
`RLL_RATE_P/D`, `PTCH_RATE_P/D` and none for yaw; none for VTOL, rover and unknown. `Finding.params` is the P
name in the P band; in the D band the D name plus, for a multirotor, `IMU_DGYRO_CUTOFF` (PX4) or
`ATC_RAT_<RLL|PIT|YAW>_FLTD` (ArduPilot). When no `ParamRecommendation` was made, the band is P or D and the
vehicle has no P gain name, the text " (No <axis> gain is suggested for this vehicle type: <frame>,
<autopilot>.)" is appended to the generic recommendation.
<<<
VERIFICATION: Test, Inspection
VERIFICATION_NOTE: >>>
Tests check the names for PX4 multirotor, PX4 fixed wing and ArduPilot multirotor and that a rover and an
unknown vehicle get no parameter change. The filter names and the appended note are checked by inspection.
<<<
ALLOCATION: analysers
RELATIONS:
- TYPE: Parent
  VALUE: HLR-DYN-005

[REQUIREMENT]
UID: LLR-DYN-017
STATUS: Active
TITLE: Mechanical band advice
STATEMENT: >>>
For a peak at `HIGH_BAND_HZ` = 35.0 Hz or above `_finding` makes no `ParamRecommendation` for any vehicle, sets
the recommendation to check props, motor mounts and flight-controller mounting and then set up a notch filter,
and sets `Finding.params` to `IMU_GYRO_NF0_FRQ`, `IMU_GYRO_NF0_BW` for PX4, `INS_HNTCH_ENABLE`,
`INS_HNTCH_FREQ` for ArduPilot and an empty list for an unknown autopilot.
<<<
VERIFICATION: Inspection
VERIFICATION_NOTE: >>>
No test puts a peak at 35 Hz or above. Inspection checks the `else` branch of the band selection in `_finding`
and the `NOTCH` table.
<<<
ALLOCATION: analysers
RELATIONS:
- TYPE: Parent
  VALUE: HLR-DYN-005

[REQUIREMENT]
UID: LLR-DYN-018
STATUS: Active
TITLE: Oscillation confidence and time window
STATEMENT: >>>
`_finding` sets `confidence` to `confidence(amp / threshold - 1, duration_s, quality)`, where threshold is the
limit of the level reached (40, 15 or 5 deg/s) and quality is the lower of 1.0 and (rate / 2) / (2 x peak_hz).
It sets the finding's time window from `t0` and `t1` through `when`, which leaves out a value that is None or
not finite.
<<<
VERIFICATION: Test, Inspection
VERIFICATION_NOTE: >>>
The test checks only that the confidence is between 0 and 1. The formula is checked by inspection.
<<<
ALLOCATION: analysers
RELATIONS:
- TYPE: Parent
  VALUE: HLR-DYN-004

[REQUIREMENT]
UID: LLR-DYN-019
STATUS: Active
TITLE: No random or clock input in the four modules
STATEMENT: >>>
`oscillation.py`, `vibration.py`, `ekf.py` and `ascent_profile.py` import no `random`, `time` or `datetime`
module and call no random generator; they iterate lists, dict items in insertion order and `sorted` keys only.
`EKFAnalyser.analyse` stores the autopilot name on the instance (`self._autopilot`) for `_advice` to read, so
one instance is not safe to run on two logs at once.
<<<
VERIFICATION: Inspection
VERIFICATION_NOTE: >>>
No test runs a check twice and compares the results. Inspection reads the import lists and every loop of the
four modules.
<<<
ALLOCATION: analysers
RELATIONS:
- TYPE: Parent
  VALUE: HLR-DYN-024

