RELATIONS:
- TYPE: Parent
  VALUE: HLR-DYN-007

[REQUIREMENT]
UID: LLR-DYN-021
STATUS: Active
TITLE: Topic instances
STATEMENT: >>>
`_instances` returns `(index, topic, frame)` in index order for every topic whose name matches the pattern
(`sensor_accel`, `vehicle_imu_status` or `vibration`, bare or with a `_<n>` suffix) and whose frame is not None
and has a `timestamp` column. The bare name is index 0; when both the bare name and `_0` are present the bare
one is used, whatever the order of the topics.
<<<
VERIFICATION: Test
ALLOCATION: analysers
RELATIONS:
- TYPE: Parent
  VALUE: HLR-DYN-007

[REQUIREMENT]
UID: LLR-DYN-022
STATUS: Active
TITLE: Counter increase in flight
STATEMENT: >>>
`counter_increase` drops non-finite values, returns 0 with fewer than two left, and otherwise returns the
integer sum of the positive steps between consecutive values where both samples are in flight. A step down is
counted as 0 (a counter restart).
<<<
VERIFICATION: Test
ALLOCATION: analysers
RELATIONS:
- TYPE: Parent
  VALUE: HLR-DYN-010
- TYPE: Parent
  VALUE: HLR-DYN-009

[REQUIREMENT]
UID: LLR-DYN-023
STATUS: Active
TITLE: Vibration source priority and skip
STATEMENT: >>>
`VibrationAnalyser.analyse` uses the IMU list of `_imu_status` if it is not empty, else that of `_vibe`, else
that of `_raw`; sources are not mixed. When all three are empty it returns `_skip("Not enough vibration or
accelerometer data <phase>.")`. `_vibe` is told the vehicle is PX4 only when `vehicle_from_params(params)`
gives autopilot `"px4"`; ArduPilot and unknown are treated alike.
<<<
VERIFICATION: Test, Inspection
VERIFICATION_NOTE: >>>
A test checks that the PX4 metric is used when raw data is also present. The skip and the treatment of an
unknown autopilot are checked by inspection.
<<<
ALLOCATION: analysers
RELATIONS:
- TYPE: Parent
  VALUE: HLR-DYN-007

[REQUIREMENT]
UID: LLR-DYN-024
STATUS: Active
TITLE: PX4 vibration metric
STATEMENT: >>>
For each `vehicle_imu_status` instance with an `accel_vibration_metric` column `_imu_status` takes the in-flight
rows and skips the instance with fewer than `MIN_METRIC_SAMPLES` = 10 finite values. The level is the
`LEVEL_PERCENTILE` = 95th percentile of the finite values in m/s2 and the limits are `PX4_LIMITS` = (4.905,
9.81). It also records the 95th percentile of `gyro_vibration_metric` when that column has a value, and the
in-flight increase of `accel_error_count` plus `gyro_error_count` as `errors`. The source is `"px4_metric"`.
<<<
VERIFICATION: Test
ALLOCATION: analysers
RELATIONS:
- TYPE: Parent
  VALUE: HLR-DYN-008
- TYPE: Parent
  VALUE: HLR-DYN-007

[REQUIREMENT]
UID: LLR-DYN-025
STATUS: Active
TITLE: Autopilot vibration topic, not PX4
STATEMENT: >>>
For each `vibration` instance on a vehicle that is not PX4, `_vibe` uses the columns present among
`vibration_x`, `vibration_y`, `vibration_z` (m/s2), takes the in-flight rows with at least one finite value and
skips the instance with fewer than 10. The series is the largest axis value per row, the level its 95th
percentile, the limits `ARDU_LIMITS` = (30.0, 60.0) and the source `"vibe"`. The per-axis 95th percentiles go
into the finding text.
<<<
VERIFICATION: Test
ALLOCATION: analysers
RELATIONS:
- TYPE: Parent
  VALUE: HLR-DYN-008

[REQUIREMENT]
UID: LLR-DYN-026
STATUS: Active
TITLE: Autopilot vibration topic, PX4
STATEMENT: >>>
On a PX4 vehicle `_vibe` reads `vibration_z` only (PX4 puts gyro metrics in x and y), applies `PX4_LIMITS` =
(4.905, 9.81) m/s2 and marks the source `"px4_metric"`. An instance without `vibration_z` is skipped.
<<<
VERIFICATION: Test
ALLOCATION: analysers
RELATIONS:
- TYPE: Parent
  VALUE: HLR-DYN-008

[REQUIREMENT]
UID: LLR-DYN-027
STATUS: Active
TITLE: Level from raw accelerometer data
STATEMENT: >>>
For each `sensor_accel` instance with x, y and z columns (`x`/`xyz[0]`, `y`/`xyz[1]`, `z`/`xyz[2]`) `_raw`
drops rows with a NaN in time or any axis, keeps the in-flight rows and skips the instance with fewer than
`MIN_RAW_SAMPLES` = 50. The sample rate is 1e6 over the median interval (250.0 Hz if it cannot be computed). It
subtracts from each axis a centred rolling mean of max(3, round(`HPF_CUTOFF_S` = 0.2 s x rate)) samples
(`min_periods` 1). The level is the square root of the mean over all rows of the sum of the three squared
residuals (m/s2), with limits `ARDU_LIMITS` = (30.0, 60.0). The series for timing and the chart is the same
quantity over a centred window of max(1, round(`RMS_WINDOW_S` = 1.0 s x rate)) samples. No minimum sample rate
is required.
<<<
VERIFICATION: Test, Inspection
VERIFICATION_NOTE: >>>
Tests check that gravity and a steady offset are not counted and that 10, 45 and about 70 m/s2 of white noise
give no finding, a warning and a critical finding. The window lengths and the absence of a rate check are
checked by inspection.
<<<
ALLOCATION: analysers
RELATIONS:
- TYPE: Parent
  VALUE: HLR-DYN-008

[REQUIREMENT]
UID: LLR-DYN-028
STATUS: Active
TITLE: Vibration level finding
STATEMENT: >>>
`_level_finding` returns None when the level is at or below the warning limit. Above the critical limit it
returns a critical finding of `kind` `vibration.critical`, otherwise a warning of `kind` `vibration.elevated`,
both of category `vibration`. The time window runs from the first to the last sample whose series value is above
the warning limit (no window when there is none). `confidence` is `confidence(level / limit - 1, n x dt)` with
limit the one crossed, n the number of samples above the warning limit and dt the median sample interval in
seconds. The text gives the level, the metric, the statistic, the limit and the peak of the series.
<<<
VERIFICATION: Test
ALLOCATION: analysers
RELATIONS:
- TYPE: Parent
  VALUE: HLR-DYN-008
- TYPE: Parent
  VALUE: HLR-DYN-012

[REQUIREMENT]
UID: LLR-DYN-029
STATUS: Active
TITLE: IMU sensor error finding
STATEMENT: >>>
`VibrationAnalyser.analyse` adds an info finding of `kind` `vibration.imu_errors` for each IMU whose `errors`
count is `IMU_ERROR_MIN` = 10 or more, with `confidence(errors / 10 - 1)`. Only the `vehicle_imu_status` source
sets `errors`.
<<<
VERIFICATION: Test
ALLOCATION: analysers
RELATIONS:
- TYPE: Parent
  VALUE: HLR-DYN-011

[REQUIREMENT]
UID: LLR-DYN-030
STATUS: Active
TITLE: Clip count sources
STATEMENT: >>>
`_clip_counts` returns per IMU index `(count, source text, from a counter, in-flight sample count)`. It takes,
in this order and never replacing an index already filled: the summed in-flight `counter_increase` of every
`accel_clipping*` column of `vehicle_imu_status` instance n for index n; the in-flight `counter_increase` of a
`clipping_<k>` column of any `vibration` instance for index k; and for a `sensor_accel` instance the result of
`_raw_clips`. Clip counts are read from all three topics whichever source gave the level.
<<<
VERIFICATION: Test
ALLOCATION: analysers
RELATIONS:
- TYPE: Parent
  VALUE: HLR-DYN-010

[REQUIREMENT]
UID: LLR-DYN-031
STATUS: Active
TITLE: Clips from raw accelerometer data
STATEMENT: >>>
`_raw_clips` returns, when the frame has `clip_counter*` columns, the sum of their in-flight values (NaN as 0)
marked as a counter. Otherwise it needs x, y and z columns and at least 50 in-flight rows without NaN (else
None), and for each axis whose largest absolute value is `CLIP_MIN_RANGE` = 75.0 m/s2 or more counts the samples
at `CLIP_PLATEAU` = 0.99 of that value or more, adding the count to the total only when it is 3 or more; the
total is marked as not from a counter.
<<<
VERIFICATION: Test
ALLOCATION: analysers
RELATIONS:
- TYPE: Parent
  VALUE: HLR-DYN-010

[REQUIREMENT]
UID: LLR-DYN-032
STATUS: Active
TITLE: Clipping finding
STATEMENT: >>>
For each IMU index in ascending order `VibrationAnalyser.analyse` adds a finding of `kind`
`vibration.clipping`. From a counter: none for a count of 0 or less, info below `CLIP_COUNTER_WARNING` = 100,
warning at 100 or more, with `confidence(count / 100 - 1)`. From the plateau fallback: none when the count is
below `CLIP_MIN_COUNT` = 10 or below `CLIP_MIN_FRACTION` = 0.001 of the in-flight samples, otherwise a warning
with `confidence(count / max(10, 0.001 x samples) - 1)`.
<<<
VERIFICATION: Test
ALLOCATION: analysers
RELATIONS:
- TYPE: Parent
  VALUE: HLR-DYN-010

[REQUIREMENT]
UID: LLR-DYN-033
STATUS: Active
TITLE: IMU disagreement
STATEMENT: >>>
With two or more IMUs in the list `VibrationAnalyser.analyse` takes the spread as the highest level less the
lowest and adds a warning of `kind` `vibration.imu_mismatch` when the spread is above `MISMATCH_FRACTION` = 0.5
times the warning limit of the first IMU (2.4525 m/s2 for the PX4 metric, 15.0 m/s2 otherwise), with
`confidence(spread / limit - 1)`. The constant `IMU_CONSISTENCY_THRESH` is not used.
<<<
VERIFICATION: Test
ALLOCATION: analysers
RELATIONS:
- TYPE: Parent
  VALUE: HLR-DYN-011

[REQUIREMENT]
UID: LLR-DYN-034
STATUS: Active
TITLE: Vibration module score
STATEMENT: >>>
`VibrationAnalyser.analyse` starts `health_score` at 100.0 and subtracts 30 for each critical and 15 for each
warning level finding, 5 for each sensor-error finding, 15 for each warning and 5 for each info clipping finding
and 15 for an IMU disagreement. The result carries the score limited to not below 0.0.
<<<
VERIFICATION: Test
ALLOCATION: analysers
RELATIONS:
- TYPE: Parent
  VALUE: HLR-DYN-012

[REQUIREMENT]
UID: LLR-DYN-035
STATUS: Active
TITLE: Vibration key metrics
STATEMENT: >>>
`VibrationAnalyser.analyse` writes `vibration_vs_limit` (level over warning limit of the IMU where that ratio is
highest, 3 places) and `total_clip_count` (sum of all counts from `_clip_counts`). When that IMU's source is
`"px4_metric"` it writes `max_accel_vibration_metric` (highest level, m/s2, 3 places) and, when a gyro metric
was read, `max_gyro_vibration_metric` (4 places); otherwise it writes `max_imu_rms` (highest level, m/s2, 3
places).
<<<
VERIFICATION: Inspection
VERIFICATION_NOTE: >>>
No test names this requirement; the behaviour is exercised only indirectly by other tests. A review reads the
key metrics built in `VibrationAnalyser.analyse`.
<<<
ALLOCATION: analysers
RELATIONS:
- TYPE: Parent
  VALUE: HLR-DYN-012

[REQUIREMENT]
UID: LLR-DYN-036
STATUS: Active
TITLE: In-flight rows
STATEMENT: >>>
`_in_flight` drops rows without a timestamp, sorts by time, drops rows where all the named columns are NaN and
returns the rows whose timestamp is inside an in-air window of `flight_phases(topics)`. `_raw`, `_clip_counts`
and `_raw_clips` apply the same in-air mask to their own rows.
<<<
VERIFICATION: Test
ALLOCATION: analysers
RELATIONS:
- TYPE: Parent
  VALUE: HLR-DYN-009

[REQUIREMENT]
UID: LLR-DYN-037
STATUS: Active
TITLE: Vibration chart data
STATEMENT: >>>
Each IMU entry holds a chart of at most 500 points made with `downsample`: `timestamps` in ms and `vibe` (the
series), the per-axis series `x`, `y`, `z` for the non-PX4 `vibration` topic and for raw data, the `warn` and
`crit` limits, and for raw data the per-axis and combined RMS. Level and sensor-error findings carry their own
IMU's chart with `instance`; clipping and disagreement findings carry `{"instances": {topic: chart}}`.
<<<
VERIFICATION: Test, Inspection
VERIFICATION_NOTE: >>>
The test checks that a finding's chart has an `x` or an `instances` key. The other keys are checked by
inspection.
<<<
ALLOCATION: analysers
RELATIONS:
- TYPE: Parent
  VALUE: HLR-DYN-012

