[REQUIREMENT]
UID: LLR-DYN-056
STATUS: Active
TITLE: GPS rows used and input skips
STATEMENT: >>>
`AscentProfileAnalyser.analyse` returns `_skip("GPS topic missing altitude or timestamp data.")` when
`vehicle_gps_position` lacks `alt` or `timestamp`. It keeps rows whose `alt` is not null, whose `fix_type` is 3
or more when that column exists, and whose `lat` and `lon` are not both 0 when both columns exist, sorted by
time. With fewer than 10 such rows it returns `_skip("Not enough GPS samples with a 3D fix to characterise a
flight profile.")`. Altitude is `alt` / 1000 in metres and time is `timestamp` / 1e6 in seconds. The rows are
not limited to armed or in-air time.
<<<
VERIFICATION: Test
ALLOCATION: analysers
RELATIONS:
- TYPE: Parent
  VALUE: HLR-DYN-020

[REQUIREMENT]
UID: LLR-DYN-057
STATUS: Active
TITLE: Single apogee
STATEMENT: >>>
`_find_apogee` returns None when the altitude range is below `MIN_PROMINENCE_M` = 5.0 m. Otherwise it calls
`scipy.signal.find_peaks` with a prominence of the larger of 5.0 m and `PEAK_PROMINENCE_FRACTION` = 0.15 of the
range and returns the index of the peak when exactly one is found, else None. On None `analyse` returns a skip
whose reason says the profile has no single dominant peak.
<<<
VERIFICATION: Test
ALLOCATION: analysers
RELATIONS:
- TYPE: Parent
  VALUE: HLR-DYN-020

[REQUIREMENT]
UID: LLR-DYN-058
STATUS: Active
TITLE: Reference altitude and qualification
STATEMENT: >>>
`AscentProfileAnalyser.analyse` takes the altitude of the first kept GPS row as the launch altitude, the apogee
height as the peak altitude less it, the time to apogee as the peak time less the time of the first kept row,
and the final height as the last altitude less the launch altitude. It returns a skip ("Single altitude peak at
<h>m AGL with no high-G boost phase ...") when the apogee height is below `MIN_QUALIFYING_APOGEE_M` = 300.0 m
and `_analyse_launch` returned no `peak_boost_g`.
<<<
VERIFICATION: Test
