[REQUIREMENT]
UID: LLR-DYN-060
STATUS: Active
TITLE: Boost detection
STATEMENT: >>>
`_analyse_launch` reads x, y, z of `sensor_accel` (m/s2), takes the magnitude, and among the samples between the
first GPS time and the apogee time finds the run of consecutive samples above `LAUNCH_G_THRESHOLD` = 2.5 x
9.80665 m/s2 with the most samples. When that run lasts `MIN_BOOST_DURATION_S` = 0.2 s or more (first to last
sample) it returns a finding of severity good, `kind` `ascent.boost`, confidence 1.0, over the run, and the key
metrics `peak_boost_g` (peak magnitude / 9.80665, 2 places) and `boost_duration_s` (2 places). It returns
nothing when a column or `timestamp` is missing, no sample is in the ascent, or no run is long enough.
<<<
VERIFICATION: Test
ALLOCATION: analysers
RELATIONS:
- TYPE: Parent
  VALUE: HLR-DYN-021

[REQUIREMENT]
UID: LLR-DYN-061
STATUS: Active
TITLE: Boost stability
STATEMENT: >>>
`_analyse_boost_stability` reads the three rate columns of `vehicle_angular_velocity` inside the boost run,
needs 5 samples or more and a mean rate magnitude of 1e-6 rad/s or more (else no result), writes the key metric
`boost_gyro_rate_cv` (standard deviation over mean of the magnitude, 3 places) and returns a warning of `kind`
`ascent.boost_asymmetric` over the boost when it is above `ASYMMETRIC_LOADING_CV_WARNING` = 0.6, with
confidence the lower of 1.0 and cv / 1.2.
<<<
VERIFICATION: Test
ALLOCATION: analysers
RELATIONS:
- TYPE: Parent
  VALUE: HLR-DYN-021

[REQUIREMENT]
UID: LLR-DYN-062
STATUS: Active
TITLE: Descent rate and deployment finding
STATEMENT: >>>
`_analyse_deployment` returns nothing with fewer than 5 samples from the apogee on or an apogee height of 0 or
less. It takes the descent rate (m/s, positive down) as the height lost between consecutive samples over their
time difference (a difference of 0 or less taken as 0.001 s) and, when there are 40 or more rates, smooths it
with a centred rolling median of int(count / 20) samples. When `_find_deployment_index` returns an index it adds
a finding of severity good, `kind` `ascent.deployment`, confidence 0.9, at that time, and the key metrics
`parachute_deployment_altitude_m` (height interpolated at that time) and `time_from_apogee_to_deployment_s`
(1 place each).
<<<
VERIFICATION: Test
ALLOCATION: analysers
RELATIONS:
- TYPE: Parent
  VALUE: HLR-DYN-022

[REQUIREMENT]
UID: LLR-DYN-063
STATUS: Active
TITLE: Low deployment
STATEMENT: >>>
`_analyse_deployment` adds a warning of `kind` `ascent.low_deployment`, confidence 0.7, when the deployment
height is below `LOW_DEPLOYMENT_ALTITUDE_FRACTION` = 0.1 of the apogee height.
<<<
VERIFICATION: Test
ALLOCATION: analysers
RELATIONS:
- TYPE: Parent
  VALUE: HLR-DYN-022

[REQUIREMENT]
UID: LLR-DYN-064
STATUS: Active
TITLE: No deployment
STATEMENT: >>>
When `_find_deployment_index` returns None, `_analyse_deployment` returns one critical finding of `kind`
`ascent.no_deployment`, confidence 0.85, with no time window, if the final height is at or below
`LANDING_ALTITUDE_FRACTION` = 0.1 of the apogee height; otherwise it returns no finding.
<<<
VERIFICATION: Test
ALLOCATION: analysers
RELATIONS:
- TYPE: Parent
  VALUE: HLR-DYN-022

[REQUIREMENT]
UID: LLR-DYN-065
STATUS: Active
TITLE: Deployment index
STATEMENT: >>>
`_find_deployment_index` returns the first index i for which the samples from time i to time i +
`DEPLOYMENT_WINDOW_S` = 3.0 s number 3 or more, the rate at i is above 1.0 m/s, the median rate of the later
half of those samples is `MIN_CANOPY_DESCENT_MS` = 1.0 m/s or more, and the rate at i divided by that median is
`DEPLOYMENT_SPEED_RATIO` = 2.5 or more. It returns None when no index qualifies, which includes every log with
fewer than 3 rate samples in any 3.0 s.
<<<
VERIFICATION: Test
